The graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-2.5,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-1,1,2,3,4} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-2,0) -- (1,3);
\fill (1,3) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[thick, ->] (1,1) -- (4,1);
\draw[fill=white] (1,1) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
At which value of $x$ is the function discontinuous?

The graph of $y = g(x)$ is shown below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (-1.5,0) -- (5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.5) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1,2,3,4,5} {\draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1); \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-1,1,2,3,4,5} {\draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left,font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <->] (0.5,0.75) -- (1.9,1.95);
\draw[thick, <->] (2.1,2.05) -- (4.5,3.25);
\draw[fill=white] (2,2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (2,4) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Classify the discontinuity occurring at $x = 2$.
